  {
    "episode_id": 72222,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72222.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, definitely, yes. I mean, for one thing, the Senate could be doing with the democracy, what the House is doing with it. I mean, we passed the For the People Act to protect early voting and weekend voting and mail-in balloting, which are all under attack by state legislatures across the country with just dozens and dozens of voter suppression schemes. But the Senate is completely hamstrung by the filibuster. I mean, we've got to lower prescription drug prices by giving the government the power in Medicare to negotiate for lower drug prices, which is a power the government's got in the Medicaid program it's got in the program again we passed it in the house but it's sitting over there in the Senate we need to act on climate change. which is bearing down upon us, which is a nightmare that is very much on the minds of young people in the country, but obviously affects everybody. So we got to be doing more with the government, but also as you suggest, we got to be doing more with our politics. My district actually has been with a blue county, a red county, Carroll County, Purple County, which is Frederick County and then Montgomery is my home county. It is because of judicial decisions now, it's been made much more homogenous and much more democratic. So just because you can make something look more compact doesn't mean you make it more competitive. And I think there's a, there's a lot of confusion about gerrymandering and we haven't even really agreed upon a definition for it. But what we fought for in the for the People Act was movement to independent redistricting panels, take the politicians out of it. And then I favor multi member districts where you get real proportional representation, the kind that you know other democratic countries It figured out, but in terms of my own campaign, I spend no money on TV radio consultants bolsters. It all goes into democracy summer, which is our educational summer project for high school kids and college kids to get involved in the defense of democracy. and democratic values and it will be going across the country this summer we should have more than a thousand democracy summer fellows working with us so yeah I'm with you on this on my side of the aisle the the blue the people representing blue districts should not just sit back and you know be content with reelection we need to mobilize the idealism and the energy within our own districts to help people in neighboring districts and in swing districts across the country. I think the Supreme Court's outrageous and impending destruction of the constitutional right to privacy gives us the opportunity to mobilize people to go vote like we've never voted before.",
