  {
    "episode_id": 72222,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72222.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, people are dealing with practical problems. So if you have diabetes, as millions of people do in the country, and your insulin is costing you $100 or $150 a month, or more $200 or $250 a month, depending on what kind and how much you have to use and so on. That's a problem that you could expect your government to deal with. I mean, our people are spending five or 10 times more than people in other countries simply because our government can't negotiate with big pharma. to regulate those prices. And we're the only country on Earth which can't. And why is that? Well, there was a guy named Billy Townsend, who was a congressman who slipped that in. And then after he left Congress the next year, became, what do you know, the president of Big Pharma? All right, there's a depressing Washington tale for you. But we have legislation, which we passed on the House side, that will allow people to get their insulin for no more than $30 a month. But it's wrapped up with the Senate and with the implacable opposition of the other party, which to my mind is lock, stock, and barrel in the thrall of big pharma.",
